DOCKER COMPOSE FUNDAMENTALS:
• Docker Compose manages multi-container applications using a YAML file.
• It enables isolated environments on a single host, preserving volume data and only recreating changed containers.
• Compose supports variables for environment portability and orchestrates container communication.
• It is ideal for development, testing, CI workflows, and staging by automating environment setup and teardown with simple commands.

DOCKER COMPOSE BEST PRACTICES:
• Use Docker Compose to efficiently orchestrate multiple containers serving a common purpose.
• Avoid repeating service options by creating reusable YAML templates.
• Include an init process in containers to prevent zombie processes.
• Manage container networking via service names or assigned IPs.
• Update containers on the fly with docker-compose up -d and force rebuilds with --build.
• Use a single Compose file for all environments and explicitly version images.
• Keep Compose files version-controlled alongside the app without secrets.
• Maintain consistent ports and environment variables centrally.
• Define named volumes at the top of the Compose file for flexibility.
• Use clear naming conventions for traceability and ensure accurate exit codes for troubleshooting.

DOCKER COMPOSE PROJECTS:
• Define your Docker Compose project by creating a Docker file and identifying dependencies.
• Build the project to download and install all necessary libraries and dependencies.
• Run the project and connect required services for proper application function.
• Use docker-compose.yml to configure services, volumes, and environment variables.
• Use docker-compose up -d to start containers in detached mode and docker-compose down to stop and clean up containers and networks.

DOCKER COMPOSE CLI:
• Docker CLI is a command line tool to interact with the Docker daemon via its REST API.
• Docker Compose is an additional CLI tool that simplifies working with Docker commands.
• The docker-compose command supports flags like -f for specifying compose files and -p for project names.
• Multiple compose files can be combined, with later files overriding earlier ones.
• Environment variables can also configure compose file locations and project names.

